# Cursor Governance Wiring Boundary

## Supersession notice

**This version (3.0.0) supersedes 2.0.0.** v2.0.0 (2026-07 update) described the
target model as "`.cursor/rules/` stays a real repository-owned directory, shared
rules project in only as selected individual file symlinks." That model was never
implemented — `ops/scripts/setup_workspace_symlinks.sh` kept creating
whole-directory symlinks for `.cursor/rules` (and the three `$HOME/.cursor/{rules,
skills,commands}` directories), so every governed repo was in violation of its own
governance rule. Rather than build the individual-symlink selector v2.0.0 called
for, this version replaces that plan entirely: governance content loads through
Cursor's own local-plugin mechanism instead of any rules-directory symlink, global
or selective. Per the Graphiti `Supersedes` convention (`03-graphiti-memory.mdc`),
this is a replacement, not a silent overwrite — v2.0.0's text is preserved in git
history, not deleted.

## The model (v3.0.0)

GlobalCommands (`$HOME/.cursor-governance`) is wired into Cursor two ways, for two
different purposes, and they must not be conflated:

| Mechanism | Path | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Governance reference plane | `.cursor-commands -> $HOME/.cursor-governance` (per workspace) | Commands, skills, scripts, canonical documents, and explicit rule references — read by name, not auto-loaded |
| Cursor plugin (rule/skill/command activation) | `~/.cursor/plugins/local/l9-governance -> $HOME/.cursor-governance` (user scope) | The `l9-governance` local plugin. Cursor discovers `rules/`, `skills/`, `commands/` under this path itself; nothing symlinks those subfolders individually |
| Per-class addon plugin | `~/.cursor/plugins/local/<addon> -> $HOME/.cursor-governance/plugins/<addon>` or returned ad hoc via `workspaceOpen` | Class-specific rule bundles (e.g. `odoo-class-addon`) loaded only in workspaces `environment/plugins/exceptions.yaml` classifies into that class — see `environment/plugins/README.md` |

`.cursor-commands` does not replace plugin-based rule activation, and the plugin
does not replace `.cursor-commands` as the reference path used by scripts/commands
that need a stable, name-addressable path to the governance repo.

## Repository path contracts

- `.cursor-commands` is the sole workspace symlink to the governance root for **consumer** workspaces. Unchanged from v2.0.0 for consumers.
- The SSOT clone (`$HOME/.cursor-governance`) must **never** have a `.cursor-commands` self-alias (realpath(workspace)==realpath(SSOT) → remove, do not create). Self-links nest forever in the IDE.
- `.cursor/plans` → `$HOME/.cursor/plans` is a required convenience symlink (machine Cursor plans; not governance SSOT). Wiring tools create/heal it; validators require it.
- Tip freshness: sessionStart runs `governance_activate_fresh.sh` so loaded governance HEAD equals GitHub `origin/main` (ff or clone+swap). Stale Dropbox/old tips are not acceptable load sources.
- `.cursor/governance/` is a real local directory; `.cursor/governance/CANONICAL_LAW.md` is a file symlink to the canonical law. Unchanged from v2.0.0.
- `.cursor/rules/` is repository-owned and reserved for rules a repository genuinely authors itself. It must **never** be a symlink — whole-directory or individual-file — to any part of GlobalCommands. This supersedes v2.0.0's "selected individual file symlinks" allowance: that mechanism is retired in favor of the plugin model above.
- Shared governance rules, skills, and commands are never symlinked into a repository's `.cursor/` tree. They activate exclusively through the `l9-governance` plugin (always-on) and per-class addon plugins (conditional).
- `$HOME/.cursor/rules`, `$HOME/.cursor/skills`, `$HOME/.cursor/commands` are likewise never whole-directory symlinks to GlobalCommands. The plugin mechanism is user-scoped through `~/.cursor/plugins/local/`, not through Cursor's native rules/skills/commands directories.
- `.cursor/commands` and `.cursor/skills` remain forbidden duplicates of the shared entry point, in a repository or at `$HOME` — unchanged from v2.0.0.

## Mutation rule

Wiring tools must preserve repository-owned directories. Unexpected symlinks,
files, and filename collisions fail closed instead of being renamed or overwritten
by assumption. A pre-existing `.cursor/rules` (or `$HOME/.cursor/{rules,skills,
commands}`) symlink left over from the v2.0.0-era script is a migration artifact,
not repository content — wiring tools remove the symlink outright (never back it
up as if it were real content) and leave the path absent for the repository to
populate genuinely, if it ever does.

## Known limitation

Cursor's `workspaceOpen` hook — the mechanism per-class addon plugins depend on —
has a documented reliability gap on some Cursor builds (hooks registered but never
executed). The `l9-governance` core plugin does not depend on this hook and loads
unconditionally via the static `~/.cursor/plugins/local/l9-governance` symlink;
only class-gated addons are affected if the hook does not fire on a given machine.
See `environment/plugins/README.md` for the adapter-level detail.
